; reload.bl — the coherent live-reload loop: every edit is a TRANSACTION over
; the running image, and the image is the FOLD of its sources through this gate.
;
; ── Truth ordering: LOG ⊐ FILE ⊐ IMAGE ───────────────────────────────────────
;
; Three things could claim to be "the truth" and each is right at its level:
;   LOG   the facts (this journal, a session's messages, an edit's verdict) —
;         append-only, basis-addressed. Undo is a basis pointer.
;   FILE  a namespace source (.bl) or a livebook (.bl.md/.bl.org) — a FOLD of
;         the log: tools own result/verdict spans, authors own the rest.
;   IMAGE what is live — the fold of every file's sources through `commit`.
; The image is never written to directly and the file is never rewritten
; behind an author's back; a structural edit appends to the log, splices the
; file at one form's span, and stages the whole namespace here — one
; transaction, one fact. (docs/live-architecture.md §2; spell-app PLAN-001.)
;
; ── The one idea ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; A `.bl` file on disk is a live handle to a running namespace. You stage edits
; into a BUNDLE (the set of changes since the last coherent image), and `commit`
; either applies the whole bundle atomically — or holds it, printing every
; reason it does not yet make sense, WITH THE OLD CODE STILL SERVING, until a
; later edit completes the bundle. Nothing half-applied is ever observable.
;
; ── Why a bundle, not a file ────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Rename a fn across its definition and its five callers: each individual save
; is incoherent (the callers dangle, or the def is gone), yet the WHOLE change
; is fine. The transaction unit must be the edit set, or a mid-rename save would
; go live with a dangling reference — exactly the `undefined var` class the
; provenance gate (aot.ex) already closes for stale beams, lifted to live edits.
;
; ── How it stays safe: coherence is STATIC ──────────────────────────────
;
; `commit` decides coherence from the bundle SOURCES plus the live image's
; public names — WITHOUT executing the bundle. This is load-bearing: evaluating a
; `defn` rebuilds its global shim module (`BeamLisp.Ns.<Ns>`), a side effect an
; env fork does NOT contain (a fork isolates the var REGISTRY, not compiled BEAM
; modules). So an eval-based "candidate image" would leak an incoherent def into
; the live module the instant it validated. The predicates — dangling ref,
; promise, type — are all statically decidable, so no evaluation is needed to
; judge a bundle. Only a bundle PROVEN coherent is then evaluated into `:global`,
; where each `defn` is a `Link.defvar` in place — an atomic hot swap. Callers
; mid-flight finish on their captured body module (never reloaded, never purged);
; the next call resolves the new one. No caller observes a missing or half-updated
; var, and an incoherent bundle never touches the live image at all.
;
; ── The unit is the NAMESPACE, not the var ────────────────────────────
;
; A staged source IS the whole namespace. A name the new source no longer
; defines is genuinely REMOVED from the image on commit — not left additively
; shadow-callable. This is 'the whole renaming ethics': rename a fn and every
; reference must move, because the old name really disappears. Coherence enforces
; both faces — a same-ns reference to a dropped name is dangling (held), and a
; dropped PUBLIC var that another live namespace still calls is a :removal error
; (held until that caller is staged too). So a rename is atomic across every
; namespace it touches, or it does not land at all.
;
; ── Coherence = predicates over shipped machinery ───────────────────────
;
;   dangling ref  — a call whose head neither the bundle nor the image provides
;                   (deferred/collect-unknown-calls, the L7 deferral layer)
;   promise       — a `(declare x)` with no later `(defn x)` in bundle or image
;   require cycle — a cycle through a staged ns in the merged require graph
;                   (source-graph/cycles, the one owner — the same answer the
;                   build fails on; a cold AOT load of one is BUG-064's deadlock)
;   type          — an inferred-type conflict (typed/check-source, sound-only)
;   message       — a queued message whose contract the bundle changed
;                   (reload.migrate, Wave 3 — attached to the bundle, not here)
;
; A message-migration attached to a bundle is what makes an otherwise-incoherent
; (contract-changing) bundle coherent again — see reload/attach and reload.migrate.

(ns reload
  (:require [env] [typed] [deferred] [source-graph :as sg]
            ; the quiet window a file save needs is `proc.tick`'s debounce; the
            ; clause is resolved at EXPANSION, so the namespace that registers it
            ; must be loaded first (see `watch-debounce` below).
            [proc.server :as ps] [proc.tick]))

;; ── the bundle: staged edits since the last coherent image ──────────────

; {:staged {ns-string → source-string}   ; edits buffered, not yet applied
;  :migration migration-or-nil           ; a reload.migrate spec bound to this bundle
;  :status :empty|:staged|:applied|:held|:blocked
;  :errors [error-map …]                 ; why a held/blocked bundle does not fit
;  :applied [ns-string …]                ; namespaces swapped by the last commit
;  :requires {ns-string → [ns-string]}   ; the require edges the image REMEMBERS
;                                         ; (what apply-bundle! last evaluated) — the
;                                         ; only graph a file-less, reload-created
;                                         ; namespace has
;  :graph {ns-string → [ns-string]}      ; the parse-once memo of SOURCE-derived
;                                         ; edges (see image-requires)
;  :generation {ns-string → hash-string} ; what the image KNOWS it is made of: the
;                                         ; program hash of every namespace a commit
;                                         ; made live (see `drift`). The image's own
;                                         ; record, because nothing below this gate
;                                         ; is asked to keep one.
(def state (atom {:staged {} :migration nil :status :empty :errors [] :applied []
                  :journal [] :requires {} :graph {} :generation {}}))

(def journal-limit 200)

;; ── the commit target: WHERE a coherent bundle is made live ──────────────
;;
;; A commit evaluates the bundle into an env. In production and normal dev that
;; env is `:global` — the one running image. But a TEST that drives reload wants
;; the commit to land in ITS OWN env (a ward fork), not the shared global image,
;; so the test is isolated AND its coherence checks (which read the same env it
;; writes) agree. This is the BEAM way to make an effectful module testable: not
;; by stubbing functions, but by injecting the RESOURCE the effect targets — here
;; the env id. Bound per-process (process dict), so concurrent runs never share
;; a target and the binding cannot leak across processes.

(def commit-target-key :reload/commit-target)

(defn commit-target
  "The env a commit makes live in THIS process — `:global` unless rebound by
   `with-target`. The injectable sink that makes reload isolatable."
  []
  (let [t (erlang/get commit-target-key)]
    (if (= t :undefined) :global t)))

(defn with-target
  "Run `thunk` with the commit target bound to `env` for its dynamic extent, then
   restore the previous target. A test (or ward) binds the target to its own fork
   so a commit lands there, never in the shared `:global` image."
  [env thunk]
  (let [prev (erlang/get commit-target-key)]
    (erlang/put commit-target-key env)
    (try (thunk)
         (finally
           (if (= prev :undefined)
             (erlang/erase commit-target-key)
             (erlang/put commit-target-key prev))))))

(defn- now-ms [] (erlang/system_time :millisecond))

; live subscribers: a set of pids to notify on every reload event, so a web
; monitor pushes a frame the instant the image changes. Mirrors
; datom.broadcast's lazily-pruned pid table — a dead pid is dropped on the next
; send, so no liveness bookkeeping is needed.
(def subscribers (atom #{}))

(defn subscribe
  "Register the calling process (or `pid`) to receive `[:reload/event ev]`
   messages on every commit outcome. Returns the pid. Idempotent."
  ([] (subscribe (erlang/self)))
  ([pid] (swap! subscribers (fn [s] (conj s pid))) pid))

(defn unsubscribe
  "Stop notifying `pid` (default: the caller). Safe if never subscribed."
  ([] (unsubscribe (erlang/self)))
  ([pid] (swap! subscribers (fn [s] (disj s pid))) :ok))

(defn- notify!
  "Send `[:reload/event ev]` to every live subscriber, pruning any dead pid. A
   non-blocking `erlang/send` per subscriber — the same cheap fan-out
   datom.broadcast uses inside the writer step."
  [ev]
  (let [pids (deref subscribers)
        alive (filter (fn [p] (erlang/is_process_alive p)) pids)]
    (when (not (= (count alive) (count pids)))
      (reset! subscribers (set alive)))
    (doseq [p alive] (erlang/send p [:reload/event ev]))))

(defn- journal!
  "Append one event to the bounded journal (newest-last), so a monitor can replay
   what the image did, and notify live subscribers so a web monitor pushes a
   frame immediately. Oldest events drop past `journal-limit` — a long-running
   image never grows unbounded."
  [event]
  (let [ev (assoc event :at (now-ms))]
    (swap! state
           (fn [s]
             (let [j (conj (:journal s) ev)]
               (assoc s :journal
                      (if (> (count j) journal-limit)
                        (vec (drop (- (count j) journal-limit) j))
                        j)))))
    (notify! ev)))

(declare status)
(declare print-errors)
(declare program-hash)

;; ── the prod policy: inspect always, MUTATE gated ────────────────────────
;;
;; The reload module is pure bl — it is in the image in EVERY environment, dev
;; and prod alike. What differs is the blast radius we allow. Reading the live
;; image (inspect / status / journal / namespaces) is always safe, everywhere.
;; MUTATING it (commit → apply-bundle!) is gated:
;;
;;   dev   (unpackaged) — allowed by default; this is the developer's own image.
;;   prod  (a drop or an assembled release) — REFUSED unless BEAM_LISP_RELOAD=1
;;         is set, so an operator opts in deliberately, per node, with a bounded
;;         window.
;;
;; The KIND of image decides, and whoever built the image declares it: the Rust
;; launcher of a drop states BL_BIN (the compound it is running from), and the
;; release launcher states BEAM_LISP_IMAGE=release. Asking whether `Elixir.Mix`
;; was loaded was the old answer, and deleting Mix would have flipped every
;; development image to production and refused mutating reloads everywhere — a
;; signal that disappears with a dependency is not a signal. A project file can
;; still force the question either way: `:env {"BEAM_LISP_DEV" "1"}` makes a tree
;; development, whatever image it runs in.
;;
;; This mirrors the Wave-1 AOT gate (BEAM_LISP_AOT_STRICT): the guarantee lives
;; in the running system, and a dangerous capability in prod is off until asked
;; for. A staged, coherent bundle that is refused is HELD like any other — the
;; edit is not lost, it simply does not go live until reload is enabled.

(defn image-kind
  "What kind of image this is — `BeamLisp.Image/kind`, which is the ONE
   implementation of the rule (the application's dev-server decision reads the
   same function, because two copies of a signal are two signals).

   Measured: BL_BIN is set in a drop and nil under `mix bl`. Nothing asks whether
   Mix is loaded, because that answer stops existing the day Mix is deleted."
  []
  (BeamLisp.Image/kind))

; A runtime override for the policy: nil ⇒ decide from environment (the default);
; true/false ⇒ force enabled/disabled. Lets an operator disable reload on a live
; dev node (a canary), and lets tests exercise the refused path without a real
; release. Set via `set-enabled!`.
(def policy-override (atom nil))

(defn set-enabled!
  "Force the reload mutation policy on (`true`) or off (`false`), overriding the
   environment decision; `nil` restores environment-based detection. Returns the
   new override value."
  [v]
  (reset! policy-override v))

(defn reload-enabled?
  "Whether MUTATING reloads (commit) are permitted in this image. An explicit
   `set-enabled!` override wins; otherwise a development image is yes, and a
   packaged one (a drop or an assembled release) is no unless BEAM_LISP_RELOAD is
   set — opt-in, bounded. BEAM_LISP_DEV forces the development answer, which is
   how a tree declares itself: `:env` in its env.bl is put into the real
   environment before a command runs."
  []
  (let [o (deref policy-override)
        env (contains? #{"1" "true"} (System/get_env "BEAM_LISP_RELOAD"))]
    (if (not (nil? o))
      o
      (or env (BeamLisp.Image/mutable?)))))

(defn declared-ns
  "The namespace a source string declares — the symbol after a leading `(ns …)`.
   nil when the first form is not `(ns …)`; a staged edit must name its ns so the
   bundle keys are stable and match the running image."
  [src]
  (try
    (let [forms (BeamLisp.Reader/read_string src)
          f     (first forms)]
      (if (and f (= :list (typed/node-tag (typed/node-form f))))
        (let [items (typed/node-items (typed/node-form f))
              head  (typed/node-form (first items))]
          (if (and (= :symbol (typed/node-tag head))
                   (= "ns" (typed/node-name (first items))))
            (typed/node-name (second items))
            nil))
        nil))
    (catch _ nil)))

;; ── staging ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn stage
  "Buffer an edit into the pending bundle. Does NOT touch the live image — the
   edit becomes visible only on a coherent `commit`. `ns` may be given, else it
   is read from the source's `(ns …)` form. Returns the bundle status map."
  ([src] (stage (declared-ns src) src))
  ([ns src]
   (when (nil? ns)
     (throw (ex-info "reload/stage: source declares no (ns …); pass ns explicitly"
                     {})))
   (swap! state
          (fn [s]
            (-> s
                (assoc :staged (assoc (:staged s) ns src))
                (assoc :status :staged))))
   (status)))

(defn attach
  "Bind a message-migration spec (reload.migrate) to the current bundle. A
   bundle that changes a live process's message contract is incoherent until a
   migration is attached — the migration is what fulfils the in-flight messages
   under the new contract. See reload.migrate for the spec shape."
  [migration]
  (swap! state (fn [s] (assoc s :migration migration)))
  (status))

(defn abort
  "Discard the pending bundle without touching the live image. The JOURNAL is
   preserved — abort discards uncommitted intent, not the record of what already
   happened."
  []
  ;; ASSOC over the live state, not a fresh literal: abort discards the BUNDLE,
  ;; and everything else in `state` is a fact about the IMAGE rather than about
  ;; the bundle — the journal, the remembered require edges, the parse-once memo,
  ;; and the generation a commit recorded. Rebuilding the map from scratch
  ;; silently dropped the last three.
  (swap! state (fn [s] (assoc s :staged {} :migration nil :status :empty
                              :errors [] :applied [])))
  (status))

(defn status
  "The current bundle: what is staged, its coherence status, and why it is held."
  []
  (let [s (deref state)]
    {:status  (:status s)
     :bundle  (vec (keys (:staged s)))
     :migration (some? (:migration s))
     :errors  (:errors s)
     :applied (:applied s)}))

;; ── coherence pass (STATIC — never evaluates the bundle) ─────────────────

; Every top-level form that BINDS A NAME in its namespace. A whole-namespace
; reload must know the complete set the new source defines — anything omitted
; would be seen as "vanished" and wrongly removed, or leave a real removal
; undetected. This is the full def-vocabulary of the language.
(def def-forms
  #{"defn" "defn-" "def" "defmacro" "defmulti" "defmethod"
    "defrecord" "deftype" "defprotocol" "defserver" "defnative"})

(defn- name-text
  "The NAME a definition binds, as a STRING.

   A name carrying metadata (`(def ^:private x …)`, `(defn ^:dynamic f …)`) is a
   `:meta` node in the reader's output, and `node-name` is documented as the
   text of a symbol or keyword node — call node-form first if the node might be
   wrapped in metadata. For a `:meta` node it hands back the NODE, not its
   text. Every consumer of a name in this namespace (bundle-defs, removed-names,
   promise-errors) compares against `Env/own_names`, which holds strings — so a
   node here makes an annotated def look like a name the new source DROPPED, and
   namespace-level replacement then UNDEFINES a var the file still defines. In a
   running app that is a `500` on every request (session/roots) or a rebuild
   that refuses to apply (kit/GANTT-*) — on every other save of that file
   (FUP-033). Peeling here, at the one place a name is produced, is the fix."
  [node]
  (str (typed/node-name (typed/node-form node))))

(defn- defn-name
  "If a form is a top-level definition, the name it binds; else nil. `defmethod`
   binds no NEW name (it extends an existing defmulti), so it contributes none."
  [node]
  (let [f (typed/node-form node)]
    (if (= :list (typed/node-tag f))
      (let [items (typed/node-items f)
            head  (typed/node-name (first items))]
        (if (and (contains? def-forms head) (not (= "defmethod" head)))
          (name-text (second items))
          nil))
      nil)))

(defn- declared-names
  "The names introduced by a `(declare a b …)` form, else []."
  [node]
  (let [f (typed/node-form node)]
    (if (and (= :list (typed/node-tag f))
             (= "declare" (typed/node-name (first (typed/node-items f)))))
      (map name-text (rest (typed/node-items f)))
      [])))

(defn- bundle-defs
  "The set of names the bundle DEFINES, per namespace: {ns → #{name …}}. A
   forward reference to a name defined later in the same bundle is therefore not
   dangling — the whole edit set is judged together, which is the point."
  [staged]
  (reduce (fn [acc pair]
            (let [ns (first pair) src (second pair)
                  names (set (filter some?
                                     (map defn-name (BeamLisp.Reader/read_string src))))]
              (assoc acc ns (into (get acc ns #{}) names))))
          {} staged))

(defn- staged?
  "Whether `ns` is part of THIS bundle — i.e. its whole source is being replaced."
  [defs ns]
  (contains? defs ns))

(defn- provides?
  "Whether `name` resolves in `ns` under NAMESPACE-LEVEL semantics.

   The distinction that makes reload namespace-level rather than variable-level:
   for a ns IN the bundle, only the names its NEW source defines count — an old
   var the new source dropped is genuinely gone, so a reference to it is dangling
   (this is 'the whole renaming ethics': rename `helper`→`helper2` and the old
   `helper` is removed, not left shadow-callable). For a ns NOT in the bundle,
   the live image is authoritative (its vars are untouched). core is always
   available."
  [defs ns name]
  (or (contains? (get defs ns #{}) name)
      (and (not (staged? defs ns))
           (not (= :error (BeamLisp.Env/fetch ns name))))
      (contains? (set (BeamLisp.Env/public_names "core")) name)))

(defn- unqualified-call-heads
  "Every unqualified, non-core, non-special same-ns call head in one source. By
   passing an EMPTY `defined` set to the deferral collector's `unknowns-in`, we
   collect ALL such heads independent of the live image — which is load-bearing
   for namespace-level reload: a call to a var the new source DROPPED must be
   seen, and it is still present in the live image (until commit), so a
   live-baseline collector would wrongly treat it as resolved."
  [src ns]
  (let [core (set (BeamLisp.Env/public_names "core"))
        forms (BeamLisp.Reader/read_string src)]
    (reduce (fn [acc form] (concat acc (deferred/unknowns-in form #{} core)))
            [] forms)))

(defn- dangling-errors
  "Dangling references in one source: same-ns calls whose head the WHOLE bundle
   does not provide under namespace-level semantics. Qualified heads
   (`other.ns/f`) resolve at their target ns and are excluded by the collector;
   cross-ns removals are caught separately by `removal-errors`."
  [defs ns src]
  (let [heads (unqualified-call-heads src ns)
        still (filter (fn [u] (not (provides? defs ns (:callee u)))) heads)]
    (map (fn [u] {:kind :dangling :ns ns :var (:callee u)
                  :msg (str "undefined var: " ns "/" (:callee u)
                            " — nothing in the bundle or image defines it")})
         still)))

(defn- promise-errors
  "Unfulfilled-promise errors: a `(declare x)` with no `(defn x)`/`(def x …)`
   binding it in the bundle or image. A promise is the static analogue of a
   queued message — a commitment the bundle must honour before it goes live."
  [defs ns src]
  (let [forms    (BeamLisp.Reader/read_string src)
        declared (mapcat declared-names forms)
        unbound  (filter (fn [nm] (not (provides? defs ns nm))) declared)]
    (map (fn [nm] {:kind :promise :ns ns :var nm
                   :msg (str "declared but never defined in this bundle: " ns "/" nm)})
         unbound)))

(defn- type-errors
  "Inferred-type conflicts for one staged source (typed/check-source warnings).
   Sound-warnings-only: an untypable form is silent, never falsely flagged."
  [ns src]
  (let [ws (:warnings (deferred/check-file-deferring src ns))]
    (map (fn [w] {:kind :type :ns ns :msg (str w)}) ws)))

(defn- removed-names
  "Names the live namespace `ns` currently OWNS but the bundle's new source no
   longer defines — the vars a whole-namespace reload will drop. Empty when `ns`
   is new (nothing to remove) or the source is purely additive."
  [defs ns]
  (let [new-names (get defs ns #{})
        live      (set (BeamLisp.Env/own_names ns))]
    (filter (fn [nm] (not (contains? new-names nm))) live)))

(defn- removal-errors
  "Cross-namespace dangling from a REMOVAL: for each var this bundle drops from a
   namespace, any live caller in ANOTHER namespace that is not itself being
   updated in this bundle. This is the cross-ns half of the renaming ethics — you
   may not silently delete a var that another live namespace still calls; the
   bundle is held until those callers are staged too (typically the rename's
   other half)."
  [defs ns]
  (mapcat
    (fn [nm]
      (let [outside (filter (fn [pair] (not (staged? defs (first pair))))
                            (BeamLisp.Env/callers_of ns nm))]
        (map (fn [pair]
               {:kind :removal :ns ns :var nm
                :caller-ns (first pair) :caller (second pair)
                :msg (str "removing " ns "/" nm " would break live caller "
                          (first pair) "/" (second pair)
                          " — stage that namespace too, or keep the var")})
             outside)))
    (removed-names defs ns)))

(defn- source-requires
  "One namespace's require edges from its source, by the one parser
   (source-graph); nil when no source resolves (an AOT-only beam)."
  [ns]
  (let [src (try (BeamLisp.Loader/source_content ns) (catch e nil))]
    (if (string? src)
      (let [[_ reqs] (sg/header src)] (vec reqs))
      nil)))

(defn- image-requires
  "The SOURCE-derived require edges of the live image, {ns → [dep …]}, from
   the `:graph` parse-once memo: parsing every loaded namespace's source on
   every commit measured as the difference between a green reload suite and
   a native crash (the per-commit full-image re-parse churned the runtime
   into a SIGSEGV under the ward — BUG-078), so a namespace's source is read
   once and re-read NEVER; the memo grows only when `Env/namespaces` names
   a ns it has not seen. What apply-bundle! REMEMBERS (`:requires`) is
   fresher than this memo and is merged by the caller — filtered to the env
   being committed into (see require-cycle-errors).

   The boundary, stated: a namespace whose file changes OUTSIDE this gate
   (edited on disk and re-loaded through the loader, never staged) keeps its
   first-parsed edges here. Dev edits flow through stage/commit — the watcher
   is the door — and the build is the backstop for loads that bypass it
   (build-plan fails on the same sg/cycles answer)."
  []
  (let [memo (:graph (deref state))
        missing (filter (fn [ns] (not (contains? memo ns))) (BeamLisp.Env/namespaces))
        fresh (reduce (fn [acc ns]
                        (let [r (source-requires ns)]
                          (if (nil? r) acc (assoc acc ns r))))
                      {} missing)]
    (if (empty? fresh)
      memo
      (let [grown (merge memo fresh)]
        (swap! state (fn [s] (assoc s :graph grown)))
        grown))))

(defn- require-cycle-errors
  "Require cycles through a STAGED namespace, as held reasons. The merged
   graph — each staged ns's NEW requires over the live image's — is walked
   from the bundle's own roots by sg/cycles, the one owner of cycle
   knowledge (the build fails on the same answer; this gate is the dev-time
   altitude of it). A cycle admitted here is BUG-064's cross-process loader
   deadlock shipped into the next cold drop, so the bundle is held until
   one edge is converted to a by-name lookup — the idiom vm.session already
   uses for bl.daemon.

   `:requires` is VM-global (the image's memory), but the image being
   committed into is the CURRENT env — under the ward, a fork. Recorded
   edges are therefore filtered to namespaces loaded HERE: a sibling fork's
   commits are not this image's truth."
  [staged]
  (let [recorded (reduce (fn [acc pair]
                           (let [n (first pair)]
                             (if (BeamLisp.Env/loaded_ns? n)
                               (assoc acc n (second pair))
                               acc)))
                         {} (:requires (deref state)))
        merged (reduce (fn [acc pair]
                         (let [[_ reqs] (sg/header (second pair))]
                           (assoc acc (first pair) (vec reqs))))
                       (merge (image-requires) recorded) staged)
        roots (vec (map first staged))
        cycs (sg/cycles roots (fn [n] (get merged n [])))]
    (map (fn [cyc]
           {:kind :require-cycle :ns (first cyc)
            :msg (str "require cycle: " (join " -> " (to-list cyc))
                      " — a cold AOT load of a require cycle deadlocks (BUG-064);"
                      " break one edge by resolving that namespace by name"
                      " (Loader/ensure_loaded + Env/fetch!) at the point of use")})
         cycs)))

(defn coherence
  "Every coherence error across the whole bundle, computed STATICALLY from the
   sources and the live image's public names. Empty ⇒ the bundle is coherent and
   may be committed. Exposed for the demos/tests to inspect a bundle without
   committing it. The message predicate is applied by `commit` via the attached
   migration (Wave 3), not here."
  [staged]
  (let [defs (bundle-defs staged)]
    (vec (concat (mapcat (fn [pair]
                           (let [ns (first pair) src (second pair)]
                             (concat (dangling-errors defs ns src)
                                     (promise-errors defs ns src)
                                     (removal-errors defs ns)
                                     (type-errors ns src))))
                         staged)
                 ; a whole-bundle property, not per-ns: the graph, from the
                 ; staged roots, once.
                 (require-cycle-errors staged)))))

;; ── commit: prove coherent, then swap atomically; else hold ─────────────

(defn- apply-bundle!
  "Evaluate every staged source into the live `:global` image. Called ONLY after
   the whole bundle is proven coherent. Each `defn` is a `Link.defvar` in place —
   an atomic per-var hot swap; a caller in flight finishes on its captured
   (never-purged) body module, the next call resolves the new one.

   Every bundle ns is marked LOADED before evaluation so a `(:require [sibling])`
   between two nses in the SAME bundle resolves to the live sibling instead of
   searching the disk for a file that does not exist — the whole edit set is one
   image, so its internal requires are satisfied by the image, not the loader."
  [staged]
  (let [defs (bundle-defs staged)]
    (env/with-env (commit-target)
      (fn []
        ;; Declare EVERY bundle namespace up front, so a qualified cross-ns call
        ;; `(other.ns/f)` compiles as a bl var reference rather than Erlang
        ;; interop — the compiler's ns-vs-interop decision (`Env.ns_exists?`) must
        ;; see the sibling exist regardless of eval order within the bundle.
        (doseq [pair staged] (BeamLisp.Env/declare_ns (first pair)))
        (doseq [pair staged]
          (let [ns (first pair)
                ;; compute the drop set BEFORE eval — eval interns the new names,
                ;; which would otherwise mask what the old image owned.
                gone (removed-names defs ns)]
            (env/eval (second pair))
            ;; THE IMAGE REMEMBERS WHAT IT IS MADE OF. The drift question asks
            ;; whether the image still matches its sources; a namespace THIS gate
            ;; made live is answered from here, because the gate is the one door
            ;; that knows the bytes it evaluated — the module the eval builds is
            ;; in memory and carries no provenance stamp to compare against.
            (swap! state (fn [s] (assoc-in s [:generation ns] (program-hash (second pair)))))
            ;; NAMESPACE-LEVEL replacement: a var the new source dropped is
            ;; genuinely removed from the image, so nothing can still reach the
            ;; stale definition. The coherence pass already proved no live caller
            ;; depends on any of these (dangling + removal errors), so this is
            ;; safe by construction.
            (doseq [nm gone] (BeamLisp.Env/undefine_var ns nm))
            ;; mark loaded AFTER a ns evaluates, so a later bundle ns that
            ;; `(:require)`s it resolves to the live sibling. Author-ordered:
            ;; a required sibling is staged before its requirer, exactly as
            ;; source files are ordered by the require DAG.
            (BeamLisp.Env/mark_loaded ns)
            ;; the image REMEMBERS this ns's require edges: a reload-created
            ;; namespace has no file for image-requires to parse, so this is
            ;; the only graph it has. (Where a source file exists, the
            ;; source-derived edge wins — sources are fresher.)
            (swap! state (fn [s] (assoc-in s [:requires ns]
                                           (let [[_ reqs] (sg/header (second pair))]
                                             (vec reqs)))))))))))

(defn commit
  "Prove the whole pending bundle coherent (statically); on success apply it
   atomically to the live image; otherwise HOLD it — old code keeps serving, the
   errors are returned, and the bundle stays staged for the next edit to complete.

   Returns the bundle status. `:status` is:
     :applied — the bundle was coherent and is now live
     :held    — incoherent (dangling / type / promise / require-cycle); or refused
                by the prod policy (reload disabled) — the old code keeps serving either way
     :blocked — a message-migration was required or rejected (Wave 3)
     :empty   — nothing staged"
  []
  (let [s      (deref state)
        staged (vec (:staged s))]
    (cond
      (empty? staged)
      (do (swap! state (fn [s] (assoc s :status :empty))) (status))

      ;; the prod gate: mutating reloads are off in a release unless opted in.
      ;; A refused bundle is HELD, not lost — identical treatment to an
      ;; incoherent one: the old code serves and the edit waits.
      (not (reload-enabled?))
      (let [err {:kind :policy :ns (first (keys (:staged s)))
                 :msg (str "reload is disabled in this image — set BEAM_LISP_RELOAD=1 "
                           "to enable mutating reloads in production")}]
        (swap! state (fn [s] (assoc s :status :held :errors [err])))
        (journal! {:event :held :nses (vec (keys (:staged s))) :errors [err]})
        (print-errors [err])
        (status))

      :else
      (commit-coherent s staged))))

(defn- commit-coherent
  "The coherence-and-apply half of commit, once the prod policy has allowed a
   mutation. Split out so the policy gate reads as one clear cond in `commit`."
  [s staged]
  (let [errors (coherence staged)
        nses   (vec (keys (:staged s)))]
    (if (seq errors)
      (do
        (swap! state (fn [s] (assoc s :status :held :errors errors)))
        (journal! {:event :held :nses nses
                   :errors (map (fn [e] {:kind (:kind e) :msg (:msg e)}) errors)})
        (print-errors errors)
        (status))
      (do
        (apply-bundle! staged)
        (swap! state
               (fn [s]
                 (-> s
                     (assoc :status :applied)
                     (assoc :errors [])
                     (assoc :applied (vec (keys (:staged s))))
                     (assoc :staged {})
                     (assoc :migration nil))))
        (journal! {:event :applied :nses nses
                   :migration (some? (:migration s))})
        (status)))))

(defn- print-errors
  "Render a held bundle's errors — one line per reason, so a developer sees the
   whole picture of why the bundle does not yet fit, not just the first failure."
  [errors]
  (println (str "reload: bundle HELD — " (count errors)
                " reason(s); old code still serving:"))
  (doseq [e errors]
    (println (str "  [" (name (:kind e)) "] " (:msg e)))))

;; ── the watcher's quiet window: the debounce, in ONE place ──────────────
;;
;; A save is many filesystem events, and the edit must be applied ONCE, from
;; SETTLED content, after the events stop. That need — "wake me after quiet" —
;; belongs to the scheduler tier, and `proc.tick` is its one implementation:
;; `tick-reset` CANCELS the armed wake and arms a fresh one, and the generation
;; counter makes a wake already in the mailbox STALE, so a burst produces exactly
;; one fire and the fire lands after the LAST event. An "arm once, ignore the
;; rest" timer (which is what `Process.send_after` alone buys) measures the quiet
;; from the FIRST event instead: a save that takes longer than the window is
;; applied mid-write, as partial content, as a HELD bundle.
;;
;; The Elixir watcher (`lib/beam_lisp/reload_watcher.ex`) owns the OS event
;; stream, the pending set and the stage→commit — the parts that must run where
;; the caller's injected apply lives — and does NO timing of its own. It forwards
;; each event here and applies what it holds when this tells it the quiet has
;; passed.
;;
;; One owner per watcher, UNNAMED: the watcher holds the pid, and a name would be
;; a second identity for one process.

(def default-quiet-ms
  "The quiet window a watcher gets when it does not say. Long enough to span an
   editor's write-tmp/rename dance, short enough that a human does not wait."
  50)

(ps/defserver watch-debounce
  (init [_]
    (ok {:owner nil :quiet (get start-opts :quiet default-quiet-ms)}))
  ;; `:once` fires without re-arming, so the wake `init` arms is the only one
  ;; until activity re-arms it — and an owner nobody has told about an event has
  ;; nothing to hand back.
  (tick (get start-opts :quiet default-quiet-ms) :ms {:once true} [st]
    (do (when (some? (:owner st)) (erlang/send (:owner st) :flush_pending))
        (noreply st)))
  (handle-info [:reload/activity owner] [st]
    (do (proc.tick/tick-reset (:quiet st) {:name 'watch-debounce})
        (noreply (assoc st :owner owner)))))

(defn watch-debounce-start
  "Start a quiet-window owner for one watcher, `quiet-ms` long. Returns its pid.
   The caller (the Elixir watcher) forwards every file event to it and applies
   what it holds when the owner says `:flush_pending`; the owner is linked to
   nobody, so its death is the caller's to notice."
  [quiet-ms]
  (start watch-debounce {:quiet quiet-ms} {}))

;; ── drain: deterministic settle point (used by the Wave 4 watcher/tests) ──

(defn event!
  "Write an event from OUTSIDE the reload machinery into the same journal, and
   push it to the same subscribers. A build is a change to the image's
   neighborhood, and a monitor that had to know about two journals would show
   half the story. `:at` is stamped by `journal!`."
  [ev]
  (journal! ev))

(defn drain
  "A synchronous barrier: returns the current status once any in-progress commit
   has settled. The watcher (Wave 4) stages on a real file save; a test saves a
   file then calls `drain` to observe the resulting image deterministically
   instead of sleeping. Because staging and commit are synchronous swaps on
   `state`, this is the current status — the barrier exists so callers have one
   honest place to read the settled image."
  []
  (status))

;; ── generation: is the live image older than its sources? ────────────────
;
;; The image is a GENERATION — a particular fold of particular source bytes into
;; a running VM — and nothing else says WHICH one. A warm image that cannot be
;; told it is stale is indistinguishable from a correct one: this session lost an
;; hour to exactly that shape (`./bl` runs a pre-compiled snapshot of
;; `priv/std/bl/*` while `priv/std/vm/*` loads from source, so half a change
;; appears to work), and FUP-084 was filed from the same shape in the daemon.
;; `docs/dev/which-bl.md` states the cut for the launcher; this is that fact for
;; the IMAGE, as a value a command or the pane can print.
;;
;; Two anchors, because the tiers answer to different clocks.
;;
;;   TOOLCHAIN (tier-0/1: `priv/boot/`, `priv/build/`, the `lib/*.ex` substrate)
;;     The key this image booted under, against the key the sources on disk
;;     compute NOW. `AOTCache/compiler_key` is memoised in `:persistent_term`, so
;;     it IS the boot generation, and `AOTCache/current_compiler_key` says in its
;;     own doc that the daemon reads it for exactly this. Warm-applying a new
;;     compiler with the old compiler's beams resident is BUG-048's deadlock
;;     class, so a moved key is a RESTART and never a warm apply — which is why
;;     this half is a class of its own rather than a per-namespace row.
;;
;;   TREE (everything above)
;;     Per namespace, against its own source, cheaply first: a source written
;;     BEFORE this image started cannot have been written after the image read
;;     it, so one `stat` settles it. A source written AFTER is a candidate, and a
;;     candidate is then VERIFIED by the drift gate's own rule — the closure hash
;;     recomputed from the live source (`AOT/ns_closure_hash`) against the hash
;;     the loaded module carries (`__bl_provenance__/0`, the stamp the emitter
;;     writes). Equal ⇒ the image holds exactly those bytes and merely the mtime
;;     moved (a checkout, a copy); different, or no stamp to compare, ⇒ REPORTED.
;;     A namespace THIS gate made live is answered from `:generation` instead:
;;     the gate is the one door that knows the bytes it evaluated, and the module
;;     it builds in memory carries no stamp.
;;
;; The blind spot is stated rather than hidden: a source-loaded namespace whose
;; file moved after the image started, with no stamp to compare, is reported as
;; drift — the image cannot prove it holds that source, and the whole point is
;; that a possibly-stale image must never read as a correct one.
;;
;; Nothing here compiles, applies, purges or reloads anything: it reads the
;; filesystem and the loaded modules and returns the question, never the build.
;; The warm half of the answer, for a tree drift, is `warm-verdict` — the SAME
;; coherence pass `commit` runs — and `warm-apply!` is that answer applied
;; through the same gate.

(defn image-start-ms
  "When this image started, in ms since the epoch — the instant every `since`
   below is measured against.

   `erlang/system_info(:start_time)` is not on every OTP (measured: `argument
   error` on this tree's erts), so this is the same fact from the other side: the
   wall clock the runtime reports having lived, subtracted from now. Reading the
   runtime SHORT would put the start LATE, which only makes more sources
   candidates — the safe direction for a question that is asked before it is
   believed."
  []
  (let [now (now-ms)
        up  (erlang/element 1 (erlang/statistics :wall_clock))]
    (if (and (number? up) (<= up now)) (- now up) now)))

(defn- file-mtime-ms
  "A file's mtime in ms since the epoch, or nil when it cannot be read. POSIX
   seconds from `:file`, not the runtime's datetime tuple: this is compared
   against a clock, and a tuple is not one."
  [path]
  (try
    (let [r (erlang/apply :file :read_file_info
                          (list path (list (erlang/list_to_tuple (list :time :posix)))))]
      (if (erlang/is_tuple r) (* 1000 (erlang/element 6 (erlang/element 2 r))) nil))
    (catch _ nil)))

(defn- program-hash
  "The hash of a PROGRAM — the bytes a namespace's source compiles from — in the
   spelling `Loader.source_hash/1` answers (`Base.encode16()`, uppercase), so a
   hash the image recorded and a hash re-read from disk are comparable."
  [src]
  (binary/encode_hex (crypto/hash :sha256 src) :uppercase))

(defn- module-stamp
  "The provenance stamp `{:hash :key}` the LOADED module for `ns` carries, or nil
   when it carries none — a namespace loaded from source has its shim built in
   memory, and an emitter with no file to hash writes a nil hash. Nil is a
   DIFFERENT fact from a mismatched stamp: it says nothing recorded what the image
   was read from, and no caller may read it as fresh.

   This is the gate's own read (`AOT.beam_provenance/1`) written in the open: the
   gate's copy is private, and a report has to be able to ask the same question."
  [ns]
  (try
    (let [st (erlang/apply (BeamLisp.Link/module_for ns) :__bl_provenance__ (list))]
      (if (erlang/is_tuple st)
        (let [h (erlang/element 1 st) k (erlang/element 2 st)]
          (if (nil? h) nil {:hash h :key k}))
        nil))
    (catch _ nil)))

(defn- closure-hash
  "The LIVE closure hash of `ns` — the drift gate's own recomputation
   (`AOT/ns_closure_hash`). Nil when it cannot be answered (no source resolves, or
   the graph kernel is not loadable in this image): the caller must treat that as
   unknown, never as fresh."
  [ns]
  (try (BeamLisp.AOT/ns_closure_hash ns) (catch _ nil)))

(defn- stamp-verdict
  "The gate's rule (`AOT.stale_by_provenance?/2`) asked of the live image: is the
   module's stamp the source on disk, or the source it was built from? `:unknown`
   when the live hash cannot be computed — which is a fact about the image, not a
   licence to call it fresh."
  [ns stamp]
  (let [now (closure-hash ns)]
    (cond
      (nil? now) :unknown
      (and (= (:hash stamp) now) (= (:key stamp) (BeamLisp.AOTCache/compiler_key))) :fresh
      :else :stale)))

(defn- source-row
  "One loaded namespace judged against the source it resolves to NOW.

   `:state` is `:fresh` (the image provably holds this source), `:moved` (the
   source moved after the image started and the image does not provably hold it),
   or `:unanswerable` (no source resolves, or the provenance question cannot be
   answered). `:why` says which way a `:fresh`/`:unanswerable` was reached, so a
   reader never has to re-derive it."
  [ns since gen]
  (let [path (try (BeamLisp.Loader/source_path ns) (catch _ nil))
        mtime (if (string? path) (file-mtime-ms path) nil)
        recorded (get gen ns)]
    (cond
      (nil? path)
      {:ns ns :state :unanswerable :why :no-source}

      (and (some? recorded)
           (= recorded (try (BeamLisp.Loader/source_hash ns) (catch _ nil))))
      {:ns ns :path path :mtime mtime :state :fresh :why :applied}

      (or (nil? mtime) (<= mtime since))
      {:ns ns :path path :mtime mtime :state :fresh :why :predates-image}

      :else
      (let [stamp (module-stamp ns)
            v (if (some? stamp) (stamp-verdict ns stamp) :stale)]
        (cond
          (= :fresh v) {:ns ns :path path :mtime mtime :state :fresh :why :verified}
          (= :unknown v) {:ns ns :path path :mtime mtime :state :unanswerable :why :no-provenance}
          :else {:ns ns :path path :mtime mtime :state :moved :stamp (some? stamp)})))))

(defn- toolchain-drift
  "Whether the toolchain this image is RUNNING is still the toolchain on disk: the
   keys it booted under against the keys the sources compute now. Both reads are
   the AOT cache's own, so a moved key means exactly what it means to the gate."
  []
  (let [booted {:compiler (BeamLisp.AOTCache/compiler_key)
                :build (BeamLisp.AOTCache/build_key)}
        live {:compiler (BeamLisp.AOTCache/current_compiler_key)
              :build (BeamLisp.AOTCache/current_build_key)}]
    {:booted booted :live live
     :moved? (or (not= (:compiler booted) (:compiler live))
                 (not= (:build booted) (:build live)))}))

(defn warm-verdict
  "What the reconcile gate says about making `nss`'s CURRENT sources live — asked
   WITHOUT staging them into the live bundle and WITHOUT committing, on a
   candidate bundle read from disk.

   `:coherent` ⇒ the drift is warmly applicable and `commit` would take it.
   `:held` names the gate's reasons, and the old code keeps serving exactly as it
   would on a refused commit. `:empty` when none of `nss` has a source on disk."
  [nss]
  (let [staged (reduce (fn [acc ns]
                         (let [src (try (BeamLisp.Loader/source_content ns) (catch _ nil))]
                           (if (string? src) (assoc acc ns src) acc)))
                       {} (to-list nss))]
    (if (empty? staged)
      {:status :empty :namespaces [] :errors []}
      (let [errs (coherence staged)]
        {:status (if (empty? errs) :coherent :held)
         :namespaces (vec (keys staged))
         :errors errs}))))

(defn- short-key
  "A cache key short enough for one line, or `?` when there is none."
  [k]
  (if (string? k) (str (subs k 0 12) "…") "?"))

(defn- drift-summary
  "The drift fact as ONE sentence, held in the read-model rather than in a
   renderer, so the terminal pane, the web view and a test print the same words
   instead of three paraphrases of them."
  [d]
  (let [c (:class d)
        tc (get-in d [:image :toolchain])]
    (cond
      (= :toolchain c)
      (str "TOOLCHAIN — the tree's compiler/driver sources moved since this image booted; "
           "it is running " (short-key (get-in tc [:booted :compiler]))
           " and the sources on disk hash to " (short-key (get-in tc [:live :compiler]))
           (if (empty? (:toolchain d)) "" (str " (also moved: " (join ", " (:toolchain d)) ")"))
           " — restart required")

      (= :tree c)
      (str "TREE — " (count (:tree d)) " source"
           (if (= 1 (count (:tree d))) "" "s")
           " moved after this image started: " (join ", " (:tree d))
           (cond
             (= :coherent (get-in d [:warm :status]))
             " — the reconcile gate accepts them (warmly applicable)"
             (= :held (get-in d [:warm :status]))
             (str " — HELD: " (:msg (first (get-in d [:warm :errors]))))
             :else " — no source on disk to apply"))

      :else
      (str "none — this image matches its sources ("
           (:fresh d) " verified, " (:unanswerable d) " with no source to check)"))))

(defn drift
  "Is this image older than the sources it serves, and what would it take to say
   no? A VALUE — a pane, a log or a test can print it — read and never applied:
   nothing here recompiles, reloads or purges.

   `opts` may carry `:since` (ms since the epoch) to ask about a window of the
   caller's own; the default is this image's start, which is the question the
   system actually has.

   `:class` is `:none` | `:tree` | `:toolchain`:
     :toolchain — the compiler or the driver moved since this image booted, so
                  the running toolchain is not the tree's: restart.
     :tree      — application sources moved after this image started. `:warm` is
                  the reconcile gate's verdict on them, and `warm-apply!` is that
                  verdict applied through the same gate.
     :none      — nothing the image can see has moved.

   `:changed` carries every reported namespace with the evidence (its source
   path, the mtime that moved it, and whether a provenance stamp existed), so the
   report never has to be taken on trust."
  ([] (drift {}))
  ([opts]
   (let [start (image-start-ms)
         since (or (:since opts) start)
         gen (:generation (deref state))
         rows (mapv (fn [ns] (source-row ns since gen)) (sort (BeamLisp.Env/namespaces)))
         moved (filterv (fn [r] (= :moved (:state r))) rows)
         tc-nses (filterv (fn [r] (BeamLisp.Tiers/toolchain_source? (:path r))) moved)
         tree-nses (filterv (fn [r] (not (BeamLisp.Tiers/toolchain_source? (:path r)))) moved)
         tc (toolchain-drift)
         warm (warm-verdict (map :ns tree-nses))
         d {:class (cond (or (:moved? tc) (not (empty? tc-nses))) :toolchain
                         (not (empty? tree-nses)) :tree
                         :else :none)
            :at (now-ms)
            :since since
            :image {:started-at start :toolchain tc}
            :changed (vec (map (fn [r] {:ns (:ns r) :path (:path r) :mtime (:mtime r)
                                        :stamp (:stamp r)})
                               moved))
            :tree (vec (map :ns tree-nses))
            :toolchain (vec (map :ns tc-nses))
            :fresh (count (filterv (fn [r] (= :fresh (:state r))) rows))
            :unanswerable (count (filterv (fn [r] (= :unanswerable (:state r))) rows))
            :warm warm}]
     (assoc d :summary (drift-summary d)))))

(defn warm-apply!
  "Make the drifted TREE namespaces live through the one gate: stage each from its
   source on disk and commit the bundle as ONE transaction, so a rename whose
   halves both moved lands atomically and a half-moved rename is HELD with the old
   code still serving.

   The warm half of FUP-084 — tree drift routed through the existing reconcile
   gate rather than a restart. It applies nothing that is not already the source
   on disk, and it cannot touch the toolchain class: a moved codegen key is a
   restart, and `drift` keeps saying so after this returns."
  []
  (let [d (drift)]
    (if (empty? (:tree d))
      (status)
      (do
        (doseq [ns (:tree d)]
          (let [src (try (BeamLisp.Loader/source_content ns) (catch _ nil))]
            (when (string? src) (stage ns src))))
        (commit)))))

;; ── inspect: the queryable live-image read-model (CLI + web monitors) ───

(defn- app-ns?
  "Whether `ns` is application code worth showing in a monitor — excludes the
   language's own runtime/system namespaces, which are infrastructure, not the
   app the developer is watching. A monitor wants to see THEIR image, not ours.

   Kept current with the W0 tier taxonomy (PLAN-072): everything under
   priv/{boot,std,lib} is infrastructure — matched by NAME for the flat
   tier namespaces and by PREFIX for the layered ones (datom.*, auth.*,
   live.*, …)."
  [ns]
  (let [infra #{"core" "user" "env" "typed" "deferred" "z3" "multi" "web"
               "interop" "sugar" "hiccup" "style" "diff" "socket" "ui"
               "reader" "reader-node" "compiler" "source-graph"
               "ns-interface" "build-plan" "build" "data-readers"
               "datom" "auth" "veritas" "loom" "minikanren" "wry"
               "rewrite" "deodorant" "codebase" "effects" "proc.flow"
               "optics" "test" "termination" "promote" "errors"}
        prefixes ["system." "reload." "live." "datom." "auth." "veritas."
                  "loom." "executor." "specter." "clojure." "bl."]]
    (not (or (contains? infra ns)
             (some (fn [p] (some? (index-of ns p))) prefixes)))))

(defn namespaces
  "Every application namespace live in the image, each with its public var names
   — the shape a monitor lists. Pass `:all` to include the language's own system
   namespaces too. Returns [{:ns :vars [name …] :count n} …], sorted by name."
  ([] (namespaces false))
  ([all?]
   (let [all-ns (BeamLisp.Env/namespaces)
         keep   (if all? all-ns (filter app-ns? all-ns))]
     (sort-by :ns
              (map (fn [ns]
                     (let [vars (sort (BeamLisp.Env/own_names ns))]
                       {:ns ns :vars vars :count (count vars)}))
                   keep)))))

(defn journal
  "The reload event journal, newest LAST: every commit's outcome (:applied /
   :held) with the namespaces it touched and — for a hold — why. The history a
   monitor replays. Optional `n` caps to the most recent n events."
  ([] (:journal (deref state)))
  ([n] (let [j (:journal (deref state))]
         (if (> (count j) n) (vec (drop (- (count j) n) j)) j))))

(defn inspect
  "A full, JSON-friendly snapshot of the live image for a monitor to render:
   the current bundle status, every application namespace with its vars, and the
   recent event journal, and whether the image is STILL the sources it serves
   (`drift` — the one field that tells a reader which generation this is). One
   call the CLI prints and the web pushes over a socket
   — the single read-model behind every view of the running system."
  ([] (inspect false))
  ([all?]
   {:status   (:status (deref state))
    :bundle   (status)
    :namespaces (namespaces all?)
    :journal  (journal 50)
    :drift    (drift)
    :at       (now-ms)}))
